Item C, also on page 4, in PrioritizeHud.tsx, which you own right now. Atahan: "I approve something but I can't see WHAT I'm approving; what are the underlying requirements?"

1. At the top of the target inspector, above the score and waterfall, add a compact block called "What you're deciding on". Fetch it from `GET /api/definition/requirement/{key}?cp=` (react-query, cached). It shows:
   - the requirement statement;
   - "From area: …";
   - the metric (name · unit · direction);
   - the rung in use for THIS line × market, with its value and basis (e.g. "Target ≤ 18 min · projected", "PM chose stretch" when a def_choice exists);
   - up to 3 acceptance criteria;
   - a "View definition →" link to /definition with that requirement selected.

   Keep it label-first and short (DESIGN.md).
2. Bundle section: today it lists bare keys. Show each member as a row with its title, its target value for this market, its own score and band, and a "lifted to <band> by the bundle" note where that applies. Clicking a row opens that member's inspector.
3. Put the same sentence next to the decision buttons as on the confirm line, e.g. "You confirm Must for '<title>' in 5 Series · US. Bundle members keep their own decisions." Make it say clearly whether a decision applies to one target only (it does: one requirement × line × market) or to the bundle.

FYI: I already fixed the "Show bundle" dimming bug in Skyline.tsx (around line 250: a shown bundle now overrides the selection dimming, and its members are drawn hot). Re-read Skyline.tsx before you edit it; it's also where your threshold planes live.

Item D, on the page-4 skyline columns (the Column component in Skyline.tsx and its labels; re-read first). Atahan: "I want my decisions to be much more visible in Prioritize, and if the AI later proposes changing my decision because of new data, that must be visible on the column itself too."

1. **PM-confirmed columns must stand out.** Today it's only a small blue disc at the base. Make it clearly the PM's position (blue is allowed here under the One Blue Rule; it is the ONLY use of blue on this page):
   - a blue cap/flag at the top of the column with the confirmed band letter and a check, e.g. "M ✓";
   - the column's outline or edge drawn in sweep blue (keep the domain hue as the fill, so domain stays readable);
   - the base disc stays.

   It must be readable at the overview zoom without opening anything. Unconfirmed (AI-only) columns stay as they are.
2. **Challenged decisions** = an open proposal against the PM's confirmed band (`against: "pm"`):
   - keep the blue PM cap at the confirmed band;
   - add a white attention marker on the column: a "!" badge plus a dashed white ghost segment or ring at the height of the AI's proposed score/band;
   - add a short label "AI: Should ▼ · new fact" (proposed band + direction + cause);
   - pulse once on arrival at the checkpoint (respect reduced motion).

   White = attention per DESIGN.md. NO amber; amber is reserved for weakened/lost. Proposals against the AI's own earlier band (no PM decision) keep today's quieter ▲/▼.
3. **Matrix view:** same language. Confirmed cells get the blue mark plus check. Challenged cells get the white "!" and the proposed band shown.
4. **Priorities list:** confirmed rows show "PM ✓" in blue, and challenged rows sort to the top with a white "!".
5. Add rows for these marks (PM-confirmed and challenged) to the page-4 Guide legend.

